Verruca Vulgaris
Common Infections and Afflictions
Exophytic rough papule, Common on hands and fingers, Thrombosed capillaries

VISIT CONTEXT
Case ID: CIA-047
Reason for Visit: Rapidly spreading, painful "growths" on the hands and fingers.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage identified a patient reporting an acute, distressing spread of hyperkeratotic lesions across both hands, with several located in the periungual region causing significant pain and restricted manual dexterity. The patient expressed severe anxiety regarding the possibility of an aggressive skin malignancy or a highly contagious viral infection. An urgent home assessment is required to perform a definitive dermatological examination, rule out amelanotic melanoma or squamous cell carcinoma, and initiate immediate chemical or physical treatment to halt autoinoculation spread. Delaying this assessment poses a risk of further physical impairment and escalating patient panic, satisfying the criteria for an urgent after-hours MBS assessment (MBS 585-600).

SUBJECTIVE
Chief Complaint: These rough, ugly lumps are spreading all over my fingers, and they hurt every time I try to type or hold a pen.
History of Present Illness: Symptoms initiated 3 months ago with a singular lesion, which has since rapidly autoinoculated to adjacent skin on both hands. The patient reports multiple exophytic, dome-shaped, and hyperkeratotic papules. The surface texture is described as rough and verrucous. While initially painless, the lesions have become symptomatic due to their location on pressure points and near the nail beds. The patient notes the disruption of normal skin lines and the presence of "tiny black splinters" inside the lumps. Denies systemic features, fever, or weight loss.
Functional Status: Independent, but currently limited in fine motor tasks due to periungual pain.
Social/Safety Concerns: Living alone. High situational distress regarding the appearance and contagiousness of the lesions.

OBJECTIVE
Vitals: BP: 122/78 mmHg | HR: 80 bpm | Temp: 36.6 °C | RR: 14 bpm | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 72 Kg
General: Alert, well-perfused. Visibly distressed, holding hands together to hide the lesions.
Recent/Relevant Labs: None available. Clinical diagnosis based on morphology and paring.
Physical Exam: Dermatological examination of the dorsal hands and fingers reveals seven distinct, exophytic, rough papules. The largest is 8mm, located on the right index finger. Lesions exhibit a classic verrucous surface and disrupt normal dermatoglyphic lines. Gentle paring reveals pathognomonic pinpoint black dots (thrombosed capillaries). No ulceration, irregular pigmentation, or friability (ruling out malignancy). No regional lymphadenopathy.
Environmental Observations: Safe residential environment.
Medication Reconciliation: No regular medications. Patient attempted to "pick" at the lesions with tweezers, exacerbating the spread.

ASSESSMENT
Primary Diagnosis: Verruca Vulgaris - Status: Exacerbated (Autoinoculation)
Differentials:
Squamous Cell Carcinoma - Ruled out by the classic viral features (thrombosed capillaries, disrupted skin lines), lack of rapid growth, and absence of friability or central ulceration.
Molluscum Contagiosum - Unlikely given the hyperkeratotic, rough surface rather than smooth, umbilicated papules with a waxy core.
Actinic Keratosis - Ruled out by the age of the patient and the exophytic, dome-shaped morphology rather than flat, sandpaper-like lesions on sun-damaged skin.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY
The patient’s acute distress and functional impairment from spreading periungual lesions necessitated an urgent clinical evaluation to exclude malignancy and stop further viral transmission. Direct physical examination confirmed a benign but aggressive presentation of Verruca Vulgaris (common warts). By performing immediate mechanical debulking and initiating a high-potency chemical treatment plan in the home, the patient’s pain was reduced and the viral load was immediately lowered. This time-critical intervention and reassurance prevented an unnecessary emergency department visit for a "spreading growth," fulfilling MBS 585-600 criteria.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN
Clinical Interventions: Performed paring of the hyperkeratotic surface to debulk the lesions and enhance medication penetration. Prescribed Salicylic acid 17% liquid to be applied strictly to the lesions nightly under occlusion (tape).
Positive Behavioral Change: To definitively stop the virus from autoinoculating to other parts of your body, you must immediately stop picking or biting the warts. Picking at the rough surface physically releases millions of viral particles into the micro-cuts on your fingers, directly causing the rapid spread you are currently experiencing.
Home-Based Services: None required.
DME/Safety: None required.
Follow-up: Review with regular General Practitioner in 2 weeks for consideration of cryotherapy (liquid nitrogen) for refractory lesions.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN
Seek medical review if a lesion begins to bleed spontaneously, changes color to a dark black or brown, or if the surrounding skin becomes hot, red, and swollen.

PATIENT INFORMATION
Disclaimer: Warts are viral; treatment is often prolonged and requires strict compliance to be effective.
Clinical Course: The pain will improve as the hard, thick skin is thinned. The warts themselves may take 4-12 weeks of daily acid treatment to completely resolve.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE
Seek immediate medical attention if you develop red streaks traveling from the fingers up your arm.

PATIENT EDUCATION
Treatment Explanation: The virus has caused your skin to grow excessively, creating its own protective "armor" of hard skin. The acid treatment slowly burns away these infected cells layer by layer until the virus is destroyed.
Actionable Steps: Every night, soak the warts in warm water for 5 minutes, then use an emery board to file away the dead white skin before applying the acid. Do not share towels or nail clippers with anyone else.

SMS INFORMATION
